okay  [Music]  I am here with Christopher Townsend the  visual effects supervisor for Marvel  Studios Captain Marvel thank you so much  for talking about very welcome  we are here in the Frank G Wells  building on the Disney lot and this is  where the magic happens right this is  where some of the magic happens the edit  Suites are out there the directors are  up there sort of everyday we're all  getting together and talking about the  film and how to make it better so there  are so many fun things to tackle in this  film in particular because you have  other world you have aliens you have  superpowers I want to talk a little bit  about Captain Marvel's powers we see in  the very first trailer her binary powers  come to life it's really really exciting  to see but there's so much to that so if  we sort of look at the first shot where  do you even begin to come up with a  concept for what that power is ah it's  uh it's so interesting every time I  start one of these movies I think I have  no idea how we're gonna do this  so you literally start with a blank  sheet of paper in terms of what we can  do you look at the comic books you look  at videogames you look at art you look  at you know you look at all these  different things we have a visual  development department here at Marvel  who works on all the character designs  for all the movies and I think that so  that one of the first things they do is  start amassing all this reference and  start illustrating and drawing up these  sort of these individual ideas in these  frames one of the things that Anna  Bowden and Ryan flack the directors of  the film that what they've brought to  the film is this sort of very  naturalistic feel so what was really  exciting was how do you make a big  superhero movie but keep it naturalistic  and keep it grounded in reality we were  fortunate enough to have the ability to  shoot brie Larson who is Captain Marvel  when she was shooting on a on a on a  test for another film we photographed  her in what we thought was some good  poses and some shooting some photon  blasts and taking into a binary power  and all this kind of stuff and we  started working on the the effects we  started to we we brought that footage  into the into the computer and we took  scans of Breed so that we can do all her  dimensions and photo  of her and we could actually build a  physical CG model or a CG model of her  that then matched her real model her  real real body we track all that to the  photography so that we now have a  digital version of that in the computer  and then we started with simulations and  sort of keyframe are over the top of  Bree and then we started working on  simulations and many many layers and  many months of work just trying to  create the look of trying to find  something which she doesn't look like  she's on fire she can't look you know  she her energy has to feel strong we  can't in we can't go over her  personality so it was really trying to  find all those bits and pieces and then  once you have that idea of what you want  it to look like you guys have put it  through all of those layers and you  start shooting how do you use practical  elements like lights and those sort of  things to help the visual effects  process it's always really tricky of  knowing how much where-where'd you use  interactive light and when don't you and  so one of the things we we desire we had  designed for the set unruhe Fabri were  these big sort of light bracelets that  she wore which is LED panels of lights  on her on her wrists and sometimes we  even put them on her body so that she  could interactively light up the  environment that she was walking through  but also she could light up her face so  a lot of her body is is then digitally  replaced or enhanced but obviously we  want to keep her face and we want to  keep the performance that she's giving  so often it's very useful to have sort  of when she's moving around with her  lights on her wrist that you have the  move the light moving appropriately well  I love it it is very punk rock and then  I have to say this shot is so beautiful  the scroll transformation that we see on  the beach it's really the first time we  get the full idea of what a scroll can  do how do you put together these shots  because clearly there are two actors  playing one role it was interesting it  was one of the first the first thing the  first transformation we did the first  shape-shifting we did and Ben  Mendelsohn's our sort of lead scroll and  when when he sort of turned up on set it  was one of the first things that Anna  and Ryan the directors spoke to him  about like okay  how do we do this how should we even you  know what what does a scroll feel when  he goes through this of this  transformation the shape-shifting is it  painful is it is it easy is it take a  lot of effort and I think so a lot of  that comes from the performance of what  the actor is going to bring to it and so  you know we decided that it yes it does  take a lot of effort it's a very  physiological thing that happens is a  very physical thing that should happen  it's not sort of a day we didn't want it  to feel like a just a morph between two  things or just a sort of a dissolve we  wanted to feel like there's things  happening in the body and one of the big  things that we wanted to do is to use  the grooves in the scrolls face the the  way they've always been Illustrated if  these big grooves and the chin and  they're sort of lines so we really  wanted to use that and have that be sort  of almost splitting apart and then the  other other character sort of bursting  out from from within so you have these  two very distinct shots and you have  this interesting component added to that  which is the movement of the camera it  seems like it must be such a long sort  of painstaking process how many  iterations does a shot like this go  through well there are there are  probably there there are dozens of  people that are working on a shot like  this it only took we had one day to  shoot it the entire sequence and perfect  yeah I know so it's always it's always a  little bit rushed when you're dealing  with light and we tried to we wanted it  was supposed to look like sunrise when  in fact it's sunset because that's the  only time we could get there for the  actual shoot so it's the actual get  somewhat chaotic when you're actually  trying to shoot this and then artists  sit down and then they're starting to  really work on it  so it's probably I think we got to  version 350 or something that we  actually that they were finally  submitting now we haven't seen all of  those versions they've done many  versions internally and that they're  turning things around very quickly  themselves but it's a phenomenal amount  of work that goes into a shot like that  and the painstaking work that goes into  individual you know the hairs the way  her hair grows halfway through the sort  of the the process I said wouldn't be  kind of fun if her hair just starts in a  bun and then just falls down rather than  just growing out of her head so it's you  making it up as you go you know when  you're thinking about it and your your  it's never sort of you don't go in with  a plan of exactly how it's gonna go you  you always you're reacting to what you  see so it's a it's a fascinating process  I have a feeling this next character got  you into a real hairy situation because  it's goose the cat cat actors I assume  have some limitations that human actors  don't how much of goose is visual  effects and how much of goose is your  wonderful cat actor Reggie Reggie well  it wasn't just Reggie so the complicated  thing was there were four cats on set  and Reggie was our hero cat but there  was another cat that was good for being  held and there was another cat that was  good for sitting and so some of the  shots are you know when the cat is  floating obviously it's not real and  because a real cat can do that but there  are other shots where the cat is on  somebody's lap I'm just sitting there  and doing cat things and a lot of that  isn't the real cat either a lot of that  is the computer-generated cat I think  there are about a hundred or so shots in  the movie of the cat and I think 70 of  them are CG so about two-thirds of the  shots are virtual  I've seen this side-by-side shot of the  VFX cat and the real-life cat I  personally cannot tell them apart how do  you tell them apart  one of them's got a collar on so that's  the big tail that's the big tail one of  them has to call it and one of them  doesn't what if you take off the color  mmm gets tricky the whole point right  that's what we're going to do and you  trying to and that was really a proof of  concept just to see you know because  obviously the the challenging thing in  this film is being able to match the  shots so that you can go from the light  the real cat to the CG cat back to a  live cat vector and it's really and you  know we we our job is to never take the  audience out of the moment so we have to  nail that and that was that was  incredibly challenging to get that I  love it through that lens so much and  you guys go see Marvel Studios Captain  Marvel in theaters now

